Owner ruling 2026-08-15: stdlib gets POSITIVE no-I/O declarations rather than
"no rows means no I/O", and hypergumbo ships an overlay for its third-party
deps that doubles as the user example.

module_completeness had held exactly ONE entry (math) since May, so
verify-claims refused every other module: a clean verdict was blocked by
itertools as surely as by requests. Measured end to end on a live self-survey,
this audit plus the overlay take unadjudicable modules 137 -> 102 and
unadjudicable CALLS 3474 -> 1898. Reconciled: stdlib 74 -> 47 is exactly the 27
declared here; non-stdlib 63 -> 55 is 5 completeness entries plus 3 cleared by
rows.

FIVE OF THE FIRST DRAFT'S 32 VERDICTS WERE WRONG, and reading the modules is
not what caught them - introspecting each module's OWN-DEFINED public callables
did:

  typing      typing.reveal_type() writes to stderr
  base64      base64.main (python -m base64) opens files and writes stdout
  shlex       shlex(instream=None) defaults to reading sys.stdin
  contextlib  chdir mutates filesystem state
  pathlib     its headline export IS the filesystem class

Refusing all five cost the single largest entry (pathlib, 243 calls) and is the
right direction for a gate whose wrong answer is a false all-clear. The probe is
published in the survey doc and marked ADVISORY, not a CI gate: it greps source
text, so it has false positives and cannot see C-implemented modules.

TWO THINGS IN THE BOUNDARY VOCABULARY DECIDED MOST REFUSALS and are easy to
miss: `logging` covers sys.stdout/sys.stderr, so a module that PRINTS does I/O
(argparse, warnings); `env_read` covers os.environ, so a module reading config
from the environment does too (os.path.expanduser).

ALSO FIXED, because the wiring could not land without it (INV-lufib): a
claims-file overlay is fanned out over EVERY language in the repo while
load_catalog refused any language mismatch, so a python overlay aborted any
repo that also contained javascript. Reproduced on hypergumbo's own tree - its
own overlay could not be wired into its own claims file, and the two examples
already shipped (python + go) could never be declared together. The fix
DISCRIMINATES: an overlay for another SHIPPED language is skipped (being asked
is a question, not an error), while `language: pyton` still raises, because
silently ignoring a typo leaves the author believing their rows applied. The
language->catalogue resolution is extracted to _catalog_path_for so both
callers share one rule instead of two copies of the alias table.

A ROW THAT MADE VERDICTS WORSE, kept in the overlay as its teaching point:
declaring tokenizers.Tokenizer.from_file under `methods:` when the call is
written on the CLASS asserts method-shaped I/O the analysis never observes,
tripping the method-starvation gate and turning all 18 claims inconclusive on a
reason unrelated to them. Corrected to `functions:` after checking the emitted
edge's construct.

FILED IN docs/surveys/, NOT docs/audits/. The first attempt was
docs/audits/0019-... and the format validator correctly rejected it:
docs/audits/ records per-value verdicts under an axis-declaration ADR in the
CANONICAL/FOLD/DEPRECATE trichotomy with a machine-checked YAML block naming
one of the three declared axes. These values are stdlib MODULES and the
verdicts are ENUMERATED/REFUSED - the carve-out docs/audits/README.md points to
docs/surveys/ for.

ONE TEST FIXTURE CHANGED FOR A REAL REASON, not to make red go green:
test_external_potential_does_not_double_prepend_module_in_dst_name used
re.MULTILINE. Declaring `re` enumerated means an unmatched re.* call is provably
not I/O, so F3 Filter 2 correctly stops reporting it and the test lost its
subject. It is about dst-name PREPENDING and silently depended on its module
being unaudited; moved to `logging`, which writes streams and will not be
enumerable.

